database\02_import_species.sql:
    242    'Protected',
    243: ⟪ 1057 characters skipped ⟫ to be fairly resident. Adult large fish can be found in estuaries and shelf waters to 100 m and also found on coral and rocky reefs, often associated with caves. Also occasionally caught in shallow (<50 m) prawn trawls on the Thukela Banks so must move onto sand-mud substrata at times. A favourite food on coral reefs and in rocky areas is spiny lobsters. A 177 cm standard length fish, caught from shore at Maui, Hawaiian Islands, contained 2 spiny lobsters and several large crabs. It is also known to eat a variety of fishes, including small sharks and batoids and juvenile sea turtles; in South African estuaries, the main prey item is the mud crab (Scylla serrata). Small juveniles are found in estuaries and sometimes in tidal rock pools. Adults feed on   lobsters, crabs, fish (including elasmobranchs). The juvenile\u2019s diet is unknown. Once a prime target for spearfishers due to its large size, trophy status and the fact that this species is inquisitive and easy to approach underwater, this species was over-exploited and has been fully protected in SA waters since 1992. Prior to its protection this species was also occasionally caught by recreational and commercial skiboat fisher
    244    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "FishBase", "formula_type": "log-linear", "coefficient": -10.96440029, "exponent": 2.999917984}]'

    458    'Sustainable, > 40% of pristine (Fishing Pressure Sustainable)',
    459: ⟪ 1064 characters skipped ⟫splacement of 40 recaptured fish in the Tsitsikamma National Park was 75 m. Adults inhabit shallow intertidal rocky shores and reefs down to 25 m. Juveniles are found in subtidal gullies and shallow (<10 m) subtidal reefs. Adults are omnivorous browsers feeding on red and green algae (62% of diet in EC) and small invertebrates. The juvenile\u2019s diet is similar to adults but with small crustaceans such as amphipods (beach fleas, sand hoppers, etc.) being more important in the diet. The Bronze Bream is a highly prized catch by shore-anglers along the southeast coast of South Africa. They are also targeted by the spearfishery and are occasionally caught in the ski-boat fishery. They are ranked third in terms of landed biomass by shore-anglers in the EC and the second most targeted species (12.5%) being 21% of the catch by spearfishers in the EC and WC.", "biology": "Reproductive style:  Late gonochorist (separate sexes), rudimentary hermaphrodite (possessing both male and female potential in the juvenile phase). Males and females mature after a non-functional intersexual stage. Breeding/spawning season: Spawning in groups with reproductive activity in the EC (Port Elizabeth) betwee
    460    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "FishBase", "coefficient": 2.2e-05, "exponent": 3.046, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

    505    'Decreasing, Vulnerable',
    506:   'Collapsed, <25% of pristine',
    507    '{"other_names": "Riverbream, River Perch, Wandering Seabream", "meristics": "Dorsal spines: 10-11; Dorsal soft rays: 10-12; Anal spines: 3; Anal soft rays: 8.", "appearance": "The River Bream is a silvery white to grey fish. The body has a compact bream shape, the front of the snout is slightly concave, and the anal fin is yellowish. Adults have yellow anal fins, and the edge of the operculum is blackish; juveniles have bluish fins. They are generally silver, but adults from brackish water are dark to almost black. The centers of the scales are typically dark. About the Species The River Bream is endemic occurring from central Mozambique and southwards to Swartvlei near Knysna. The distribution is not certain as this fish has been confused with the Picnic Seabream, Acanthopagrus berda. Because of the difficulty in differentiating the two fishes, anglers may not be able to identify which one they have caught. They are resident and an estuarine-dependent species with only a marine egg and larval phase. The River Bream is found mainly in estuaries and in brackish water, occasionally entering the lower stretches of freshwater rivers. Adults undertake an annual spawning migration down to the estuary mouth to spawn. Adults tolerate a wide range of salinities and turbidities and are rarely found far from estuaries in the marine environment. Juveniles less than 1 year old enter KZN estuaries at 10-50 mm SL, between Jul-Dec. Egg and larval development takes place at sea. The adult\u2019s diet is wide and includes amphipods (crustaceans with no carapace), polychaetes (bristle worms), bivalves, crustaceans, gastropods (snails, whelks and slugs), aquatic plants and small fish. The juvenile\u2019s diet from 20-60 mm consists mainly of amphipods (crustaceans with no carapace), planktonic copepods (small aquatic crustaceans), chironomid (midge) larvae, tanaids (bottom-dwelling marine and brackish-water crustaceans) and aquatic plants. In SA estuaries River Bream is harvested by recreational boat and shore anglers and by subsistence fishers using lines, fish traps and gillnets. It is a particularly important component of the recreational and subsistence catch in the three large northern KZN estuaries, Kosi Bay, St Lucia and Richards Bay and the Durban Harbour.", "biology": "Reproductive style: Assumed to be a protandrous hermaphrodite (changes sex from male to female). Breeding/spawning season: In KZN, occurring from Apr-Jul, with peak spawning possibly occurring in May-Jun. Also records exist of peak spawning in Durban Harbour in Jul-Aug, with spawning starting as early as March. Breeding/spawning locality: It occurs in nearshore marine environments, primarily in estuary mouths but seldom in the sea.  Spawning recorded in several large KZN estuaries including Kosi Bay, St Lucia, Richards Bay and Durban Bay. Age at maturity: Combined sexes: 3.6 years. Length at maturity: Combined sexes: 23 cm TL or 20 cm FL.", "citation": "NC James, BQ Mann In: Mann BQ. (Ed). Southern African Marine Linefish Species Profiles. Special Publication, Oceanographic Research Institute, Durban 9: 179-180 and Coastal Fishery Resource Guide, SAIAB, FOSAF. For more information: http://speciesstatus.sanbi.org/assessment/last-assessment/1013/"}',

    554    'Optimally exploited, 40-50% of pristine (Fishing Pressure Sustainable)',
    555: ⟪ 470 characters skipped ⟫ubtropical species found between southern MOZ and False Bay and recently also found along the southeast coast of Madagascar. Their movement patterns are unknown but data from the ORI Tagging Project (<1% recaptured from 648 tagged) suggests they may be quite nomadic with a mean distance travelled of 30km. It is believed that its movement behaviour is more likely to be fairly resident, typical of other inshore reef fish species. Adults are inshore reef fish found in shallow, rocky areas with turbulent surf while juveniles occupy shallow subtidal gullies and rock pools. The adult\u2019s diet includes mainly red and green algae but also feeds on copepods (small crustaceans) and other small invertebrates associated with algae. Juvenile\u2019s feed similar to adults with no apparent change in diet with increase in size. Stone Bream are among the top six most important species caught in the shore fishery along the KZN and Transkei coasts, becoming progressively less abundant further south. They are commonly caught by subsistence fishers and larger specimens are occasionally taken by spearfishermen.", "biology": "Stone Bream are gonochoristic (separate sexes) with an extended breeding/spa
    556    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "FishBase", "coefficient": 1.4e-05, "exponent": 3.08, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

    578    NULL,
    579: ⟪ 257 characters skipped ⟫pines: 3; Anal soft rays: 10.", "appearance": "Two conspicuous vertical black bars across the head; dorsal fin with wide black margin, especially distinct in distal spinous part of dorsal fin in fish < 30.36 cm SL, proximal part of dorsal fin yellow; hind margin of caudal fin with narrow black edge in fish to around 30 cm SL. Two rows of molars along each side of upper jaw; outer molar teeth row extends to rear end of both jaws.", "about": "This species is considered to be endemic to the Western Indian Ocean. The depth range is two to 50 m among coral reefs and enters bay and estuaries. Acanthopagrus catenula occurs in small schools and its diet primarily consists of molluscs and other benthic invertebrates.", "biology": "A. catenula is protogynous (individuals developing initially as immature females, then typically as mature females before later potentially changing sex to become a mature male) with 50% age at maturity about 4.6 years at 26.3 cm for females and 2.0 years at 21.9 cm for males. It can reach 50 cm in length but is more commonly found to be 35 cm. This species has been estimated to live to ten years. Additional research is needed to determine population trends and qu
    580    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "FishBase", "formula_type": "log-linear", "coefficient": -10.15195066, "exponent": 2.910000367}]'

    962    NULL,
    963: ⟪ 387 characters skipped ⟫ the western Indian Ocean including Pakistan, southern Oman, Kenya, MOZ and the eastern seaboard of SA extending south to Mossel Bay. They are residents however, a small proportion of the population (<5%) appears to be more nomadic with movements of up to 90km. Adults inhabit high profile reefs to depths of 75 m and are found in caves and under ledges during the day and are active nocturnally, often solitary. Juveniles are found in intertidal rock pools and subtidal gullies. Adults feed on crustaceans (shrimps, mantis shrimps, crabs) and small fish. The juvenile\u2019s diet is unknown. They form a minor component of catches in all fishery sectors in which they occur, contributing between 0.07 (shore fishing) to 1.5% (recreational boat fishing) of catches by number , except for subsistence fishing for which the contribution is not known. They are frequently taken by spearfishers in KZN. Their mean size has remained stable at ~350 mm FL. This species is potentially vulnerable to overexploitation. Regular linefish surveys and on-going catch and effort monitoring is required to provide basic catch information.", "biology": "Their reproductive style is unknown. Their breeding/spawning s
    964    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "FishBase", "coefficient": 0.0249, "exponent": 2.96, "formula_type": "power", "result_unit": "g"}]'

