   1659: ⟪ 1821 characters skipped ⟫ter like a washing machine. Adults are primarily piscivorous, feeding on species such as anchovies, sardines, and grunts but also feed on crustaceans and cephalopods (octopus and squid). Juvenile Elf <10 cm consumes more crustaceans than fish. Elf is the most important species caught by recreational shore anglers along the entire eastern seaboard of SA. They account for approximately 28-80% of the annual reported catch in the recreational KZN shore fishery and also caught in fair numbers by subsistence shore fishers in the EC and by the beach seine fishery in the WC. They may be caught and sold in unlimited numbers by the traditional commercial boat-based fishery in all provinces except KZN where sale is prohibited. Generally, they are not taken in great numbers by boat-based fisheries except those operating in areas such as False Bay and Struisbaai in the WC. They are also taken as a bycatch in the KZN prawn trawl and inshore demersal trawl fisheries and occasionally by spearfishermen. Sharks, tunas, and billfish are typically the only fish predators large and fast enough to prey on adult Elf. Elfmake up a major part of the diet of Shortfin Mako Sharks and are also very important 
   1660    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 1.5e-05, "exponent": 2.98, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

