   2499: ⟪ 671 characters skipped ⟫mottled or with 7-9 crossbars on flanks.", "about": "The Galjoen, SA\u2019s national fish, is endemic to southern Africa occurring from southern Angola to Durban and is more abundant in cooler waters <21\u02daC but rarely found on the KZN south coast. Most fish are resident (90%) however a small percentage is nomadic. Nomads can move throughout the range. It is uncertain whether individuals adopt one or the other pattern i.e. residency or nomadism, or if all fish can adopt both patterns, but in unequal proportions. Adults favour broken surf (white water) in areas of mixed rock and sand but are also found in the surf-zone along sandy beaches and in kelp beds and sometimes found on offshore pinnacles in large shoals. Juveniles inhabit the immediate sublittoral (always submerged at low tide) surf-zone. Adults are epilithic (off the rock) feeders, principally molluscivores (a carnivorous animal that specialises in feeding on molluscs such as snails, bivalves, brachiopods - marine animals that have a pair of shells, which they can clamp shut for protection, with brown mussels dominating the diet and cephalopods (octopus or squid). The diet of juveniles is unknown. Galjoen are among the 
