   3100    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "FishBase", "coefficient": 3e-05, "exponent": 2.885, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

   4634    'Stock Status: Heavily Depleted - 1.3% of pristine spawner biomass; Fishing Pressure: Heavy',
   4635: ⟪ 3615 characters skipped ⟫also found in estuaries (mainly turbid and most often in the lower/middle reaches). Adults are primarily piscivorous but also feed on cephalopods (octopus and squid) and crustaceans. Small juveniles (<50 mm TL) feed primarily on mysids (shrimps) and calanoid copepods (zooplankton, small crustaceans). Larger juveniles feed mainly on fish and mysids (shrimps). Diet also includes other crustaceans such as crabs, etc. Dusky Kob is one of the most important species in the recreational (estuarine and coastal shore angling as well as estuarine and inshore boat fishing), commercial and subsistence fisheries. They were found to be the primary target species in the EC recreational shore fishery and is prominent in the recreational shore fishery along the entire east coast of SA (Transkei and KZN), particularly in estuaries. Juveniles and sub-adults (<1100 mm TL) are commonly targeted by estuarine and surf-zone anglers, while adults are caught mainly by commercial and recreational ski-boat fishermen beyond the surf-zone. There is specific targeting of aggregations of these adult fish by ski-boats during late winter and spring in KZN, although surveys often don''t reflect this as catches are landed at night. Due to the similarity of the three kob species found in SA waters (i.e. Dusky, Silver and Squaretail Kob), regional differences in the regulations exist, particularly for boat-based fishers offshore. Due to the lack of enforcement and compliance, the bag and size limit restrictions are considered to be relatively ineffective. If precautionary measures advanced do not promote recovery, then a moratorium (a zero bag limit) and a shift to catch and release angling would be the only other option to ensure recovery of this collapsed stock.", "biology": "Reproductive style: Gonochorist (separate sexes). Breeding/spawning season: Oct-J
   4636    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 8e-06, "exponent": 3.0397, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

