   4659: ⟪ 345 characters skipped ⟫esh Dusky Kob. Silver Kob have a dark blotch at the base of the pectoral fins that lack scales. Anterior part of lateral line slightly curved. Caudal fin shape changes with growth; it may be pointed (<10 cm SL), elongate and S-shaped (10-22 cm SL), or strongly to slightly S-shaped (>30 cm). Caudal fin of larger specimens occasionally wedge-shaped to slightly rounded or truncate. Swim bladder is carrot-shaped with 31-42 arborescent appendages. Colour: In life, body silvery becoming green/brown above and white below. Dorsal surface with a copper to bronze sheen which may be very pronounced on head; dorsal and caudal fins pale yellow grey to pale grey/brown; anal, pelvic and pectoral fins from almost white to brown/grey. Mouth lining varies from pale yellow to yellowish grey; inner aspect of operculum grey to black, sometimes with a yellow tinge. In general Silver Kob have longer pectoral fins than Dusky Kob. If the end of the pectoral fin exceeds past the end of the pelvic fin it is a Silver Kob. This is more distinct in older specimens. The other distinguishing characteristic is a thicker caudal peduncle (area between the body and tail) for the Dusky Kob. The base of Silver and Dusk⟪ 408 characters skipped ⟫sciaenid species (a family of fish commonly called drums or croakers in reference to the repetitive throbbing or drumming sounds they make) in South Africa. These migratory fish can be found inshore (<60 m) in summer dispersing further offshore in winter in response to oceanographic patterns. Adults inhabit mostly moderate/low profile reef in 20-120 m depth in SEC and SC and 2-120 m in the SWC. Juveniles are found on sand/mud substrata, recruiting into shallow embayments (5-10 m depth) but moving offshore with growth. Adults feed on pelagic fish, shrimp and squid while the juvenile\u2019s diet is unknown. Silver Kob is an important component of the commercial ski-boat fishery in the SWC, SC and SEC and a sought-after species for many recreational boat-based anglers, particularly in the SEC. In South Africa it is predominantly caught between Cape Point and East London. Between Cape Agulhas and the Kei River, they are only caught by ski boat anglers and trawlers in deeper water and not in estuaries or in the surf zone. In the colder waters along the Cape west coast and Namibia they are caught in the surf zone. It is also an important component of the bycatch of inshore trawlers along
   4660    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 6e-06, "exponent": 3.07, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

