   4683: ⟪ 268 characters skipped ⟫der the \u2018chin\u2019. The swim bladder is hammer-shaped with 14-15 appendages.", "about": "Small Kob are distributed in the western Indian Ocean from Kenya to SA; south to East London in shallow (<50 m) shelf waters and estuaries and also off the west coast of Madagascar. This species was previously referred to as Johnius dussumieri, which only occurs east of Pakistan. Movement patterns are unknown but juveniles in estuaries seem to be partially estuarine-dependent while the movement of adults are thought to move out to sea for spawning, although it is reported to conduct some spawning in Lake St Lucia. Adults inhabit offshore soft substrata, turbid water and are very common on Thukela Bank and in Lake St Lucia. Juveniles are found in estuaries and offshore soft substrata in turbid water and are common on Thukela Bank and in Lake St Lucia. Adults feed on mainly crustaceans and small fish but the juvenile\u2019s diet is unknown. Small Kob are very common in shallow water prawn trawl catches on the Thukela Bank although effort in this fishery is currently extremely low. Also commonly caught by estuarine recreational boat anglers in Lake St Lucia but effort in this fishery has als
