   4730    'Collapsed, ~17% of pristine',
   4731: ⟪ 900 characters skipped ⟫f Africa from Xai-Xai in MOZ to Port Elizabeth in EC in waters less than 100 m and rarely found south of Port St Johns. They are apparently fairly resident.  Adults inhabit low relief, offshore reefs <100 m and congregate in large shoals around deep reefs and pinnacles while juveniles occupy relatively shallow <50 m offshore waters over soft substrata moving to reefs on reaching maturity. Adults eat mainly fish, some cephalopods (octopus and squid) and crustacean while juveniles eat mainly crustaceans. Squaretail Kob comprised a substantial component of commercial ski-boat catches in northern KZN during the 1980s, but catches declined sharply during the 1990s. They are also caught by recreational skiboat anglers, mainly between Thukela and Richards Bay on the KZN north coast. Juveniles used to be a fairly common bycatch in Thukela Bank prawn trawl catches especially between Jan-Feb. Given the extreme declines in CPUE and despite uncertainties in the per-recruit assessment, the stock probably collapsed in late 1980s as a result of commercial overfishing of a relatively small stock, exacerbated by prawn trawling bycatch. Concerningly, eggs (presumed to be) of this species have not been observed off Park Rynie since 2002, despite regular collecting.", "biology": "Reproductive style: Gonochorist
   4732    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 2e-05, "exponent": 2.89, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

