   4850:   'Vulnerable, <14% of pristine',
   4851    '{"other_names": "Garrick, Leerie", "meristics": "1st Dorsal spine: 7-8 (with only their bases connected by a membrane); Dorsal soft rays: 19-21; 2nd Dorsal spine with 1 spine and 19-21 soft rays; anal fin with 2 detached spines followed by 1 spine and 17-21 soft rays.", "appearance": "Diagnosis: body elongate, moderately deep and compressed; dorsal profile of head almost straight, ending in a pointed snout; eyes relatively small; upper jaw slender and rounded posteriorly, ending below or beyond posterior eye margin. Pectoral fins short; scales small, those on breast oval to strongly lanceolate, and partially embedded in the skin; lateral line very irregular and wavy, describing a convex curve over, and a concave curve behind pectoral fins; no lateral-line scutes. Colouration: Brown above, silvery white below lateral line; lobes of dorsal and anal fins black distally; juveniles (to at least 12 cm fork length) with brownish black bars on sides.", "about": "The global distribution of Leerfish is confined to parts of the Mediterranean and Black Sea, along the west coast of Africa including Mauritania to northern Namibia and along the south-eastern seaboard of SA extending all the way through to Cape Vidal and possibly on to Maputo.  They are scarce between Cunene Mouth and Table Bay. Studies have shown that the Namibian/Angolan stock is separate from the SA stock. Adults migrate annually to KZN during winter to spawn. During summer adults return to Cape waters. Adults are found in nearshore surface waters from the surf zone to a depth of 50 m and seldom found more than 500 m from shore. Juveniles are common in EC and WC estuaries which provide important nursery areas, and coastal shallows. Adults and juveniles are primarily piscivorous while smaller juveniles also feed on crustaceans. Although highly sought after as a recreational trophy fish by shore anglers, ski-boat anglers and spearfishers, especially during the winter/spring season in KZN, they are also targeted in estuaries in the EC. They make up a relatively small percentage of the overall catch of these sectors.", "biology": "Leervis are gonochoristic (separate sexes). Adult leervis undertake an annual migration to spawn in KZN coastal waters during austral winter from Sep-Nov. After spawning, eggs and larvae are transported southwards inshore of the Agulhas Current, where juveniles recruit into estuaries in the Eastern and Western Cape during summer. They remain in these nursery habitats for 1 to 3 years, before moving to the coastal marine environment to join the adult population. Males and females mature at age 4 when about 80 cm FL. The maximum recorded age is 10 years, the maximum recorded weight is 32.2 kg and the maximum recorded length is 180 cm TL.", "citation": "BQ Mann, WM Potts In: Mann BQ. (Ed). Southern African Marine Linefish Species Profiles. Special Publication, Oceanographic Research Institute, Durban 9: 179-180 and Murray TS, Duncan MI, Winkler AC, Childs A-R, Mann BQ, Potts WM (eds). 2020. Linefish resilience in the Anthropocene: The Proceedings of the 5th Southern African Marine Linefish Symposium. Rhodes University, Makhanda, 113pp. FishBase"}',

   5162    'Unknown (IOTC: Not overfished, subject to overfishing) (ICCAT: Overfished and overfishing)',
   5163: ⟪ 3901 characters skipped ⟫rvival.", "biology": "Blue Marlin are a heterosexual, dimorphic species with females growing considerably larger than males. The breeding/spawning season occurs in summer-autumn in southern latitudes < 30\u00b0S in the Indian Ocean but may spawn year-round in equatorial waters < 10\u00b0S. Distinct breeding populations and spawning occurs between 30\u00b0N and 30\u00b0S but specific spawning grounds have yet to be identified in the Indian Ocean. The maximum recorded age is 18 years for males and 27 years for females. The maximum recorded weight is 636 kg (Brazil, IGFA). SA angling record is 521.4 kg; (Sodwana, SADSAA). A further report states that males reach a maximum of 150 kg, while females may reach over 906 kg. Male Blue Marlin rarely exceed 136 kg. The maximum recorded length is 450 cm TL; 350 cm EFL.", "citation": "RP van der Elst, BQ Mann In: Mann BQ. (Ed). Southern African Marine Linefish Species Profiles. Special Publication, Oceanographic Research Institute, Durban 9: 179-180, FishBase, IGFA"}',
   5164    '[{"measure": "LBFL", "reference": "FishBase", "variant": "Atlantic", "coefficient": 0.0054, "exponent": 3.07, "formula_type": "power", "result_unit": "g"}, {"measure": "LBFL", "reference": "FishBase", "variant": "Pacific", "coefficient": 0.0054, "exponent": 3.07, "formula_type": "power", "result_unit": "g"}]'

   5402    NULL,
   5403: ⟪ 1192 characters skipped ⟫ds, climate change, increased hydropower demands and freshwater abstraction in West Coast estuaries, juveniles of obligate estuary-dependent fish such as Flathead Mullet have declined in abundance to <10% of pristine level and are likely to decline to <5% under future flow projections. Adult\u2019s diet consists of benthic microalgae, detritus and small invertebrates while larvae feed on zooplankton with the switch to a juvenile/adult diet occurring at about 20 mm SL. In SA this species is mainly caught in illegal estuarine gillnet fisheries such as in the northern parts of Lake St Lucia. They are also caught in the artisanal trap fishery in Kosi Bay and the beach seine-net fishery off Durban. Seldom caught on line but is often caught using cast nets and used for bait.", "biology": "Although this species is gonochoristic (separate sexes) it is also capable of exhibiting non-functional hermaphroditic characteristics. Breeding/spawning season: Limited data, but appears to have an extended spawning season between autumn and spring (May-Nov) in SA waters. Breeding/spawning locality:  There is evidence that spawning along the east coast of SA takes place in the marine environment, often in the vicinity of estuary mouths. Age at maturity:
   5404    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "FishBase", "coefficient": 0.0138, "exponent": 2.98, "formula_type": "power", "result_unit": "g"}]'

   5522    'Stock Status: Heavily Depleted; Fishing Pressure: Heavy',
   5523: ⟪ 2236 characters skipped ⟫lankton. Bioturbation by the sand-prawn reduces availability of benthic diatoms as food. Juveniles feed on zooplankton, benthic diatoms, surf-zone phytoplankton and detritus. Diet of post larval fish <20 mm consists mainly of copepods and macruran (crustacean) larvae. They are the main target of the gillnet fishery on the west coast, landing up to 1 500 mt per annum. Also they are a substantial illegal fishery landing more than 400 mt per annum, especially in the Berg Estuary. The legal gillnet fishery in the Olifants Estuary lands approximately 100 mt per annum. Also forms the main target of the beach-seine fishery on the west coast, current landed catches up to 1 000 mt per annum. They are also caught by the recreational, commercial, artisanal and subsistence linefisheries mainly by cast net but also by line and bottle trap and mostly used as live bait. They were a legal catch of the west coast purse-seine fishery until the 1980s. Nowadays, they are limited to incidental catches. There is a proposed experimental small-boat purse-seine fishery to assess its merits as an alternative to the gillnet fishery. Limited use as live bait (caught by cast net) by the tuna pole fishery, also
   5524    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "FishBase", "coefficient": 4e-05, "exponent": 2.7478, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

