   5642    'Collapsed, ~20% of pristine',
   5643: ⟪ 650 characters skipped ⟫ved species endemic to South Africa occurring from St Helena Bay in WC to Thukela River in KZN. The main stock is assumed to be between southern KZN and False Bay. They are migrants. Early juveniles (<150 mm) are resident in the intertidal and shallow inshore zone.  Juveniles and sub-adults (150 mm-550 mm FL) are resident in the inshore zone, while a proportion of the adult population (>600 mm FL) are thought to undertake a seasonal eastward spawning migration to Transkei and southern KZN. Adults inhabit high-profile, inshore reefs down to 20 m while juveniles are found in intertidal rockpools, gullies and shallow <12m subtidal reefs. Adults feed on gastropods (snails, whelks and slugs), echinoids (sea urchins), chlorophytes (green algae), crustaceans and polychaetes (bristle worms). Juveniles eat gastopods (snails, whelks and slugs), ascidians (sea squirts), echinoids (sea urchins), amphineurans (chitons), pelecypods (bivalves like oysters, clams, mussels, and scallops) and paguirids (hermit crabs). White Musselcracker are an important target species in the recreational shore fishery and spearfishery. They are occasionally captured in the subsistence shore fishery but seldom in the coastal ski-boat fishery. The sale of this species is prohibited and it is therefore of no commercial importance. White Musselcracker are an extremely important trophy fish for both the recreational shore fishery and spearfishery. Their limited inshore distribution, large size, slow growth, late maturity, longevity and aggregation during spawning events make this species extremely susceptible to overfishing. As with 
   5644    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 1.8e-05, "exponent": 3.037, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

