   5931: ⟪ 1002 characters skipped ⟫uite large on seaward and clear outer lagoon reefs, exceeding 75 individuals. Newly settled juveniles are found in branching coral habitats (primarily Acropora) in sheltered lagoons. Small juveniles (<50 mm) are often associated with Damselfish. Larger juvenile Humphead Parrotfish are found in lagoons, often in seagrass beds, and the adults are found in clear outer lagoons and seaward reefs up to a depth of 30 m. They feed on benthic algae and live corals. Adult Humphead Parrotfish may ram its head against corals to facilitate feeding. Each adult fish ingests over five tons of structural reef carbonates per year, contributing significantly to the bioerosion of reefs. The fish sleeps among corals, in caves and shipwrecks at night, usually in large groups.", "biology": "The fish are oviparous with distinct pairing during breeding and spawn pelagically near the outer reef slope or near promontories, gutters, or channel mouths during a lunar cycle, usually spawning just prior to the new moon and make use of spawning aggregation sites. Max length: 130 cm TL; Common length: 70.0 cm SL; Max. published weight: 46.0 kg", "citation": "FishBase, The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species, Wikipe
