   7779    '{"other_names": "Captain Fine", "meristics": "Dorsal spines: 11; Dorsal soft rays: 14; Anal spines: 3; Anal soft rays: 8.", "appearance": "Caudal fin truncate, corners rounded in adults, posterior edge convex in juveniles. Head, body, dorsal and caudal fins pale brown, with numerous small dark brown spots; ventral parts of head and body without spots: on juveniles, many of the spots merge to form double spots; prominent dark brown streak along upper edge of maxillary groove. Margin of interspinous dorsal-fin membranes yellow or gold; soft dorsal and anal fins dusky distally, with prominent white edge; upper and lower edges of rear part of caudal fin also white-edged; pectoral fins yellowish or reddish orange.", "about": "Whiteedged Rockcod are endemic and found on the south-east coast of Africa from Quissico (MOZ) to East London (EC). They appear to be resident based on limited tag-recapture data (ORI Tagging Project). Likely to be a northward movement of pre-adults from southern to northern KZN, as no spawning occurs south of Durban, although considerable numbers of small, reproductively inactive fish are caught in southern KZN. Adults inhabit deeper (>50 m) rocky reefs, to at least 120 m. The juveniles\u2019 habitat is unknown, probably reefs >30 m as seldom seen by divers. Adults feed on crustaceans, fish and squid while the juvenile\u2019s diet is unknown. Whiteedged Rockcod are reasonably common and are of interest to anglers but makes a fairly small contribution to ski-boat catches in KZN i.e. 0.9% and 2% by weight to recreationals and commercials respectively; slightly higher contribution (3-5% by weight) to semi-industrial lineboat catches in southern MOZ.", "biology": "Reproductive style: Probably protogynous (born female and changes sex to male). Breeding/spawning season: Summer, Oct-Feb. Breeding/spawning locality: Deep reefs off northern KZN and southern MOZ. Age at maturity: Females: 3.1 years; Males: derived from females; southern MOZ. Length at maturity: Females: 341 mm TL; Males: derived from females with a minimum observed length of 591 mm TL; southern MOZ. Maximum recorded age: 15 years; male; northern KZN. Maximum recorded weight: 13.0 kg. Maximum recorded length: 100.0 cm TL; male, northern KZN.", "citation": "ST Fennessy, BQ Mann In: Mann BQ. (Ed). Southern African Marine Linefish Species Profiles. Special Publication, Oceanographic Research Institute, Durban 9: 179-18, FishBase"}',

   7898    'Optimally exploited, 40-50% of pristine; Not overfished',
   7899: ⟪ 528 characters skipped ⟫ted from southern Madagascar and Mauritius, although these records are doubtful. The distribution of stock is primarily between Cape Point and Port St Johns. They are resident with small home ranges (<100 m linear extent). Adults inhabit deeper high- and low-profile inshore and offshore reefs to 100 m while juveniles are found on shallow subtidal reefs to 30 m. Adults feed on mainly echinoderms (starfish, sea urchin, or sea cucumbers), (sea lilies and feather stars and brittlestars) and cephalopods (octopus and squid) with some crustaceans (decapods - shrimp, crab, or lobster) and polychaetes (bristle worms). Juveniles eat mainly (bristle worms) with some small-bodied decapods (shrimp, crab, or lobster) and mysids (shrimp-like crustaceans). Roman are important to commercial and recreational ski-boat fisheries in WC and EC and are frequently speared by divers and occasionally taken by shore anglers fishing in deep water.  Despite a decline in total commercial catch between 1985 and 2007, Roman have sustained fishing pressure better than many other species of endemic sparid reef fish. It has been suggested that this is largely due to the small size at maturity (~180 mm FL) compared t
   7900    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 1.2e-05, "exponent": 3.154, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

