   8523: ⟪ 790 characters skipped ⟫ Beira, Mozambique to Algoa Bay, South Africa, that occurs to 120 m depth. Anecdotal evidence suggests a shrinking in the historical range with the catch of Scotsman in southern Mozambique comprising <1% of the total linefish catch. This species is both commercially and recreationally targeted and exhibits life history characteristics that exacerbate its vulnerability to overexploitation. Juveniles of this species are found on shallow rocky reefs from 10-30 m depth along the southern Kwazulu-Natal and Eastern Cape coasts, while adults are found on deeper rocky reefs from 20 m-120 m. This species is primarily a piscivore, feeding on small reef fish species, but its diet also includes benthic cephalopods and crustaceans. Juveniles are fairly resident on shallow reefs but recent tagging results suggest that adult can fish move northward >300 km and into deeper water, which may be for breeding purposes, while juveniles appear to be fairly resident, with a linear home range size of ~760 m. Numbers have decreased considerably due to overfishing.", "biology": "Scotsman is believed to be a protogynous hermaphrodite but this has yet to be confirmed. It is estimated to attain more than 13 ye
   8524    '[{"measure": "FL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 5.89e-05, "exponent": 2.81, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

   8641    'Increasing, Endangered',
   8642:   'Collapsed, <25% of pristine. Severely depleted. Fishing pressure unknown. Protected since 1998',
   8643    '{"other_names": "Seventyfour, Sev, 74 Seabream, 74, Seventy Four", "meristics": "Dorsal spines: 12; Dorsal soft rays: 10; Anal spines: 3; Anal soft rays: 8-9.", "appearance": "The Seventy-four is a beautiful fish, with a pinkish head and belly, silver-yellow body and four to six iridescent blue stripes running horizontally across its sides. On each side, above its pectoral fin, is a conspicuous black spot.", "about": "The Seventyfour is endemic to the south-eastern seaboard of SA from Cape Point in the WC to the mouth of the Limpopo River in southern MOZ. Through overfishing the former range of Seventyfour has contracted to the area between Sodwana Bay and the central Agulhas Banks with the centre of adult distribution being deep offshore reefs (>40 m depth) between Durban and East London. While juvenile Seventyfour appear to be fairly resident (ORI Tagging Project, unpubl. data), adults undertake an annual migration from Cape waters into Transkei and southern KZN during winter to spawn. This migration could also be in response to the seasonal migration of one of their prey, the sardine, into KZN waters. There is also the possibly of a return migration of the adults to the Cape after spawning but this has not been confirmed. Adults frequent deep offshore reefs (40-160 m) forming dense shoals, often found in association with ledges and pinnacles.  Juveniles are found on offshore reefs deeper than 20 m in the EC and WC. Adults are primarily piscivores feeding on pelagic species such as sardines and mackerel but their diet also includes reef fish, cephalopods (octopus and squid) and crustaceans. Juveniles feed mainly on invertebrates such as mysids (various small, shrimplike crustaceans). Throughout the 20th Century Seventy-four was intensively targeted by commercial and recreational linefishing vessels (firstly by lineboats and later by ski-boats), especially on their spawning grounds in southern KZN contributing up to 70% of linefish catch. By the late 1960s the fishery in KZN, which was made up predominantly of large adult fish, had collapsed and only relatively small quantities continued to be caught in the EC. Catches in the SE and S Cape also declined considerably. A stock assessment was conducted in 1996 based on historical data collected during 1962-63 which showed that stocks had already collapsed by the 1960s. Based on this evidence and the fact that CPUE had declined by more than 90% throughout its distribution, a moratorium was placed on the catching of Seventyfour in 1998 to allow stock rebuilding to take place. A reassessment of the Seventyfour stock was conducted in 2007, 10 years after the implementation of the moratorium. This study showed little evidence of recovery of the adult spawning population in KZN accept that there was a slight increase in mean size of adult fish. There was however, evidence of inceasing numbers of juveniles in the EC suggesting that stock rebuilding is taking place. A rolling yearly moratorium is now in place until further scientific evidence, currently being undertaken, is available.", "biology": "Reproductive style: Rudimentary hermaphrodites (the female reproductive organs come to maturity before the male) and thus functional gonochorists (separate sexes). Breeding/spawning season: Late winter to spring between Jul-Nov, peaking in Aug-Oct. Breeding/spawning locality: Offshore reefs in 50-100 m depth off the KZN south coast and northern Transkei coast. The Illovo Banks just south of Amanzimtoti on the KZN south coast was a well-known spawning aggregation site that was heavily fished by lineboats operating out of Durban Harbour after the 2nd WW. Spawning was also known to occur on deep reefs off Umvoti mouth on the KZN north coast. Age at maturity: Combined sexes: 8.8 years; KZN. First maturity is evident at 4-5 years. Length at 50% maturity: Combined sexes: 650-750 mm TL; KZN. Maximum recorded age: May be well over 20 years; KZN. Maximum recorded weight: 16 kg; SA; World angling record (IGFA 2012); SA angling record is 14.1 kg caught in 1973 (SADSAA). Maximum recorded length: 100 cm TL.", "citation": "BQ Mann, ST Fennessy In: Mann BQ. (Ed). Southern African Marine Linefish Species Profiles. Special Publication, Oceanographic Research Institute, Durban 9: 179-180, FishBase, Two Oceans Aquarium. For more information: http://speciesstatus.sanbi.org/assessment/last-assessment/1033/"}',

