   9003: ⟪ 2563 characters skipped ⟫er elasmobranchs (sharks) and cephalopods (octopus or squid) supplement their diet. Crustaceans, birds and gastropods (snails, whelks and slugs) have also been recorded in a few specimens. Juveniles (<130 cm PCL) seem to have a more varied diet with fewer elasmobranchs being consumed. Scalloped Hammerhead Sharks are commonly caught as a bycatch by recreational shore anglers and forms an important component of the competitive shore fishery in KZN and the former Transkei, but most are released. They are occasionally caught offshore by commercial and recreational ski-boat anglers and also in estuarine embayments. They comprises a large percentage (21%) of the elasmobranch bycatch taken by inshore prawn trawls off the Thukela Bank during summer, however, due to an extended closed trawl season between Aug-Feb and diminishing inshore prawn catches, the impact of the inshore prawn trawl fishery has diminished. They are the third most numerous shark caught (~166.year) in bather protection nets off certain KZN beaches. Both artisanal fisheries operating inshore and foreign vessels offshore (longliners) off MOZ target Scalloped Hammerhead Sharks for its flesh and fins. A small percentage (<0.6%) are caught as bycatch in the tuna and Swordfish longline fishery off SA. They are threatened by commercial fishing, mainly for the shark fin trade.", "biology": "Reproductive style: Viviparous with a yolk-sac placenta (eggs are fertilised and hatch inside the female shark but the embryos lack a placental connection to the oviduct or uterus and so do not feed off the mother). Breeding/spawning season: The gestation period is around 9-12 months, with pupping in spring and summer (Oct-Mar). Breeding/spawning locality: Adult females move inshore to give birth to live young (pups), who then remain inshore during their early life stages; Thukela Bank in northern KZN and other similar shallow nursery areas.  Certain coastal locations in the Transkei (northern EC) are of importance to juvenile and subadult Scalloped Hammerhead populations year-round. Age at maturity: Males: 4.3 years; Females: 5.8 years; Mexican Pacific. Length at maturity: Males: 161.5 cm PCL; Females: 183.1 cm PCL; KZ
