   9555: ⟪ 907 characters skipped ⟫e endemic to southern Africa from central Transkei to northern NAM, extending to southern Angola. They are mainly distributed from the WC to the EC in shallow waters. They prefer shallow marine reefs <50 m but may penetrate into marine dominated estuaries e.g. Kromme Estuary. They are mostly residents although a few records of movements of over 200 km have been recorded (ORI Tagging Project). Adults prefer inshore rocky reefs <30 m, may penetrate estuaries that are effectively marine inlets. Juveniles occur on shallow reefs along the SE and S Cape coasts. Adults feed on crustaceans (crabs), cephalopods (octopus and squid) and reef fish. Juveniles eat mainly rock crabs. Spotted Gully Sharks are mainly taken by recreational shore anglers, especially in competitions, but are generally released. This endemic shark is distributed close inshore in temperate waters. Late age at maturity and low reproductive capacity make this species highly vulnerable to overfishing.", "biology": "Reproductive style: Aplacental viviparity (embryos develop inside eggs that are retained within the mother''s body until they are ready to hatch), 5-15 pups. Breeding/spawning season: Mating and fertilization from Oct to early Dec. Estimated gestation period is 20 months, the entire reproductive cycle may be 2-3 years. Breeding/spawning locality: WC to the EC in shallow waters <30 m. High concentrations of small juveniles have been observed near East London. Pupping probably occurs along much of the S Cape coast. Age at maturity: Males: 10.9 years; Females: 15.3 years; SE Cape. Length at maturity: Males: 1320 mm TL; Females: 1450 mm TL; SE Cape. Maximum recorded age: 26 years; SE Cape. Maximum recorded weight: Male: 18 kg; Female: 36 kg; SE Cape. Angling record is 36.4 kg (SASAA).", "citation": "MJ Smale, C da Silva In: Mann BQ. (Ed). Southern African Marine Linefish Species Profiles. Special Publication, Oceanographic Research Institute, Durban 9: 179-180, Two 