  10203: ⟪ 664 characters skipped ⟫KZN. Here they form an important prey animal for a large number of other species. There is some indication that juveniles in the Cape are more abundant in shallow water near Mossel Bay with most fish <100 mm FL caught at depths less than 20 m. Their small mouths are equipped with several sharp incisors and two rows of molars, which they use for hunting. Adults feed on reef and sand associated bottom dwelling invertebrates including small crustaceans, echinoderms (starfish, sea urchin, or sea cucumber), polychaetes (bristle worms), fish and mollusks. The juvenile\u2019s diet is unknown. It is a widespread, abundant species which is currently not targeted by, or does not feature greatly in, any fishery and is well protected in several MPAs.", "biology": "Sand Soldiers appear to be gonochorist (separate sexes). Breeding/spawning season: In KZN spawning is during winter-spring, peaking between Aug-Sep. Breeding/spawning locality: In KZN, based on observations of eggs and larvae, spawning occurs in shelf waters.  Larval Sand Soldier drift as plankton in these waters or sink below the continental shelf to depths of up to 2km so spawning could also occur on the shelf edge. Age at maturity
