  10419: ⟪ 597 characters skipped ⟫ reduced and now is seldom caught in KZN and WC waters with the core of the remaining adult stock occurring in the former Transkei and Border regions of the EC. Tagging data has shown that juveniles (<700 mm FL) are resident on reefs in the WC and southern parts of the EC. On attaining maturity, adults migrate eastwards to the former Transkei and southern KZN. The existence of an annual eastward spawning migration is indicated by sequential catch seasons along the Cape and Transkei coast but there is no evidence of a return migration. An offshore migration has also been proposed between the inshore and offshore Agulhas Banks but lacks confirmation from tagging data. Adults inhabit offshore rocky reefs in 50-160 m between Cape Agulhas and southern KZN. Juveniles are found in shallower inshore reefs (10-50 m) between Cape Point and Kei Mouth. Adults are apex predators on deep reefs feeding mainly on reef fish such as cheilodactylids (butterfish, fingerfins), clinids (blennies) and small sparids (sea breams and porgies). They also feed on squid, octopus and crustaceans such as slipper lobster. Small juveniles feed on mysids (various small, shrimplike crustaceans), carids (crustaceans)
