  10490:   'Endangered, <6% of pristine',
  10491: ⟪ 740 characters skipped ⟫White Steenbras exist as a single, well-mixed population. The main portion of the stock is located inshore and in estuaries along the EC and WC coasts. White Steenbras are migratory. Small juveniles (<50 mm) enter estuarine nursery grounds along the Cape coast between September and November, remaining there for up to 2-3 years before re-entering the marine environment. Late juveniles (post-estuarine), (>150 mm) are resident in surf zone of sandy and mixed shores. Sub-adults (>500 mm FL) begin to undertake large-scale migrations up to 400 km, although a large proportion remains resident.  After about five years they mature at a length of 650 mm TL, when they commence annual migrations. During autumn and early winter these mature fish migrate eastwards to near the north-eastern limit of their distributional range to eastern EC, to spawn during late winter. Although not empirically confirmed, not all mature fish migrate. The return migration takes place during spring and large numbers of mature fish arrive in the South-Western Cape during summer. Many fish return to the Western Cape where they spend the summer. Adults inhabit the inshore marine environment to depths of 25 m and range 
  10492    '[{"measure": "TL", "reference": "Visboekie", "coefficient": 1.67e-05, "exponent": 2.984, "formula_type": "power-mm", "result_unit": "g"}]'

